Findings
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. THE SERVER OPENING BURST TOTALS CONFIRM cover.go, BUT MICROSOFT'S RECORD
   BOUNDARIES DO NOT.

     cloudflare   S 1221, 6, 64            = 1291   3 records
     google       S 1221, 6, 64            = 1291   3 records
     microsoft    S 1221, 6, 32, 74        = 1333   4 records

   ServerHelloResumedLen (1221), ChangeCipherSpec (6) and both ServerOpeningBurst
   totals (1291 / 1333) match exactly. But microsoft's encrypted remainder of
   106 B arrives as TWO records, 32 then 74.

   twiddle emits it as ONE: handshake.go writes
   writeSized(contentHandshake, nil, Cover.ServerEncryptedWire()) with
   ServerEncryptedWire() = ServerOpeningBurst - 1221 - 6 = 106. So against a
   microsoft cover we put 3 server records on the wire where the real identity
   puts 4, with the same byte total. The total is what CoverProfile models; the
   sequence is what an observer counts. cloudflare and google match at 3.

   CoverProfile needs the remainder as a record SEQUENCE, not a single int.

2. NewSessionTicket IS NOT SENT AT ALL ON A RESUMED CONNECTION BY TWO OF THE
   THREE COVERS.

     cloudflare   0 records unprompted
     google       0 records unprompted
     microsoft    1 record, 303 B, at 21.5ms

   twiddle sends one record of sessionTicketWire = 370 on every connection. For
   a cloudflare or google cover that record has no counterpart in the real
   identity's behaviour -- it is not a size mismatch, it is a record the real
   server does not send. For microsoft the size is wrong: 370 against a
   measured 303.

   Caveat worth keeping: this measures UNPROMPTED traffic in a 700ms window. A
   server could issue tickets later, or only after application data. Microsoft's
   arrived at 21.5ms, so the window is not obviously too short, but a variant
   that sends a request first would firm this up.

3. A REAL TLS 1.3 CLIENT SENDS A 24-BYTE close_notify. twiddle SENDS NOTHING.

   All three teardowns show the client emitting exactly one 24 B record at
   close: 5 header + 2 alert + 16 AEAD tag + 1 inner content type. twiddle's
   Conn.Close() is `return c.raw.Close()` -- a bare FIN, no alert.

   So every twiddle connection ends with a record sequence no TLS endpoint
   produces, on the LAST record of the connection, which is as cheap for an
   observer to watch as the first. This was a hypothesis before this run; the
   24 B figure is now measured.

   The servers emitted nothing at teardown here, but that is an artefact: we
   close first and stop reading 150ms later, so a server close_notify would
   often not be captured. Server-side teardown is still unmeasured.

CAVEAT ON THE CLIENT SIDE -- DO NOT USE THIS RUN TO REVISE ClientFlight
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This probe is Go's crypto/tls, not Chrome. Its client records here are

     cloudflare   C 6, 58   = 64
     google       C 6, 58   = 64
     microsoft    C 6, 74   = 80

against cover.go's ClientFlight of 149 / 145 / 164, which came from Chrome
captures (harvest/testdata/tls13-burst-resumption.log). The gap is the client,
not the model: Go's opening hello is also 1711/1761/1806 here versus Chrome's
1715 in the burst log. Go is a valid probe for what the SERVER does, because
the server's profile follows the negotiated parameters. It is the wrong
reference for what the CLIENT emits, so 149/145/164 stand and the client-side
record split remains unmeasured -- that needs a Chrome capture via
cmd/records or cmd/capture.

Reproduce:

  go run ./cmd/postflight
  go run ./cmd/postflight -hosts www.microsoft.com -quiet 2s
